{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3763 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9fc8d7e736a6db8df90a013f008188854dc0d6583868ab50c2cf0241937d6272" :edb/db-before-t 3762 :edb/db-after-t 3763 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537075 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:51:10.603Z" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186377976 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186377976 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n- **I.** $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$\n- **II.** $¬X∨(X∧¬Y)$\n- **III.** $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186377976 1074 "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬X∧(X∨¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬X∨(X∧¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$ and $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186377976 1074 "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬X∧(X∨¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬X∨(X∧¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$ and $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$; $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378181 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $E?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378181 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $E?$\n- **I.** $F∧(F∨E)$\n- **II.** $E∧(F∨E)$\n- **III.** $E∨(F∨E)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378181 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nE∧(E∨F)≡E.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $F∧(F∨E)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F∧(F∨E) & ≡F≢E.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $E∧(F∨E)$ is logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E∧(F∨E) & ≡E∧(E∨F) \\\\  & ≡E.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $E∨(F∨E)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $E∧(F∨E)$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378181 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nE∧(E∨F)≡E.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F∧(F∨E) & ≡F≢E.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E∧(F∨E) & ≡E∧(E∨F) \\\\  & ≡E.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378188 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $G?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378188 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $G?$\n- **I.** $(H∨G)∧G$\n- **II.** $H∧(H∨G)$\n- **III.** $(G∨H)∨G$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378188 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nG∧(G∨H)≡G.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $(H∨G)∧G$ is logically equivalent to $G.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(H∨G)∧G & ≡G∧(H∨G) \\\\  & ≡G∧(G∨H) \\\\  & ≡G.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $H∧(H∨G)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $G.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H∧(H∨G) & ≡H≢G.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(G∨H)∨G$ is *not* logically equivalent to $G.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(H∨G)∧G$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378188 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nG∧(G∨H)≡G.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $G.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(H∨G)∧G & ≡G∧(H∨G) \\\\  & ≡G∧(G∨H) \\\\  & ≡G.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $G.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H∧(H∨G) & ≡H≢G.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $G.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378195 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $A?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378195 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $A?$\n- **I.** $¬B∧(A∨¬B)$\n- **II.** $(¬B∨A)∧A$\n- **III.** $A∧(¬B∨A)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378195 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nA∧(A∨¬B)≡A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $¬B∧(A∨¬B)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬B∧(A∨¬B) & ≡¬B∧(¬B∨A) \\\\  & ≡¬B≢A.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(¬B∨A)∧A$ is logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬B∨A)∧A & ≡A∧(¬B∨A) \\\\  & ≡A∧(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡A.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $A∧(¬B∨A)$ is logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧(¬B∨A) & ≡A∧(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡A.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(¬B∨A)∧A$; $A∧(¬B∨A)$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378195 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nA∧(A∨¬B)≡A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬B∧(A∨¬B) & ≡¬B∧(¬B∨A) \\\\  & ≡¬B≢A.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬B∨A)∧A & ≡A∧(¬B∨A) \\\\  & ≡A∧(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡A.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧(¬B∨A) & ≡A∧(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡A.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378202 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378202 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C?$\n- **I.** $¬D∧(C∧¬D)$\n- **II.** $C∧(¬D∨C)$\n- **III.** $(¬D∨C)∧C$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378202 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC∧(C∨¬D)≡C.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $¬D∧(C∧¬D)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement $C∧(¬D∨C)$ is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∧(¬D∨C) & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(¬D∨C)∧C$ is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬D∨C)∧C & ≡C∧(¬D∨C) \\\\  & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $C∧(¬D∨C)$; $(¬D∨C)∧C$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378202 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC∧(C∨¬D)≡C.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∧(¬D∨C) & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬D∨C)∧C & ≡C∧(¬D∨C) \\\\  & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378209 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $M?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378209 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $M?$\n- **I.** $(L∧M)∨M$\n- **II.** $M∧(L∧M)$\n- **III.** $M∨(L∧M)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378209 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nM∨(M∧L)≡M.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $(L∧M)∨M$ is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(L∧M)∨M & ≡M∨(L∧M) \\\\  & ≡M∨(M∧L) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $M∧(L∧M)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $M.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement $M∨(L∧M)$ is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M∨(L∧M) & ≡M∨(M∧L) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(L∧M)∨M$; $M∨(L∧M)$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378209 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nM∨(M∧L)≡M.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(L∧M)∨M & ≡M∨(L∧M) \\\\  & ≡M∨(M∧L) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $M.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M∨(L∧M) & ≡M∨(M∧L) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378216 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $¬D?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378216 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $¬D?$\n- **I.** $¬D∨(C∧¬D)$\n- **II.** $¬D∨(¬D∧C)$\n- **III.** $(¬D∧C)∧¬D$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378216 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\n¬D∨(¬D∧C)≡¬D.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $¬D∨(C∧¬D)$ is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(C∧¬D) & ≡¬D∨(¬D∧C) \\\\  & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $¬D∨(¬D∧C)$ is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(¬D∧C) & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(¬D∧C)∧¬D$ is *not* logically equivalent to $¬D.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $¬D∨(C∧¬D)$; $¬D∨(¬D∧C)$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378216 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\n¬D∨(¬D∧C)≡¬D.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(C∧¬D) & ≡¬D∨(¬D∧C) \\\\  & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(¬D∧C) & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $¬D.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378223 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $L?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378223 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $L?$\n- **I.** $L∨(¬M∧L)$\n- **II.** $(L∧¬M)∨L$\n- **III.** $(¬M∧L)∨L$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378223 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nL∨(L∧¬M)≡L.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $L∨(¬M∧L)$ is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L∨(¬M∧L) & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(L∧¬M)∨L$ is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(L∧¬M)∨L & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(¬M∧L)∨L$ is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬M∧L)∨L & ≡L∨(¬M∧L) \\\\  & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $L∨(¬M∧L)$; $(L∧¬M)∨L$; $(¬M∧L)∨L$." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378223 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nL∨(L∧¬M)≡L.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L∨(¬M∧L) & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(L∧¬M)∨L & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬M∧L)∨L & ≡L∨(¬M∧L) \\\\  & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378230 1071 "![](images/ca/ca165438c64994306acd6031d5f8e5a0e6d4d0d11a5f6fea8097b19f5105438c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=3$\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$\n- It is continuous at $x=3$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378230 1071 "![](images/ca/ca165438c64994306acd6031d5f8e5a0e6d4d0d11a5f6fea8097b19f5105438c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378230 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(3)=-1$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, II is true, and I and III are false." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378230 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(3)=-1$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=3$” and “It is continuous at $x=3$” are false." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378327 1071 "$\\,$ **If** you need an umbrella, **then** the sky is not clear.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378327 1071 "$\\,$ **If** you need an umbrella, **then** the sky is not clear.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n- **I.** If the sky is clear, then you do not need an umbrella.\n- **II.** If you do not need an umbrella, then the sky is clear.\n- **III.** If the sky is not clear, then you need an umbrella.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378327 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement I is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378327 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement “If the sky is clear, then you do not need an umbrella” is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “If you do not need an umbrella, then the sky is clear” is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “If the sky is not clear, then you need an umbrella” is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If the sky is clear, then you do not need an umbrella” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378348 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $S$ containing $2\\times 2$ matrices that are not invertible. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186378348 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $S$ containing $2\\times 2$ matrices that are not invertible. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $S$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $S$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $S$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186378348 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ each contain a row consisting only of zeros, so they are not invertible. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis invertible. So, $S$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix that is not invertible then $de