nt, we get\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(¬P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬P∧¬Q$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379174 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $¬P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬(¬P)∨Q,$ which in turn is equivalent to $P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬P⇒Q≡P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(¬P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379309 1071 "$\\,$ **If** $8$ is even **then** $4\\in {2,4,8}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379309 1071 "$\\,$ **If** $8$ is even **then** $4\\in {2,4,8}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n- **I.** If $4\\in {2,4,8},$ then $8$ is even.\n- **II.** If $4\\notin {2,4,8},$ then $8$ is not even.\n- **III.** If $8$ is not even, then $4\\notin {2,4,8}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379309 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- 