 the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379418 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379418 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- **II.** $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **III.** $-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379418 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379418 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379604 1071 "$$\nx(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379604 1071 "$$\nx(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n- **I.** $x=-3$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379604 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+4y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=0,$ since both denominators at $x=0$ equal zero. So, $x=0$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379604 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+4y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=0,$ since both denominators at $x=0$ equal zero. So, $x=0$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$; $x=0$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379611 1071 "$$\n(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379611 1071 "$$\n(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ are analytic about $x=-3.$\n- **II.** The functions $(x+3)P(x)$ and $(x+3)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-3.$\n- **III.** The point $x=-3$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379611 1074 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-3,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-3.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}\n$$\n\n is *not* analytic about $x=-3$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-3.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-3$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-3$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186379611 1074 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-3,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-3.$ So, statement “The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ are analytic about $x=-3.$” is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}\n$$\n\n is *not* analytic about $x=-3$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-3.$ Therefore, statement “The functions $(x+3)P(x)$ and $(x+3)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-3.$” is false.\n\n- Since statement “The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ are analytic about $x=-3.$” is false, $x=-3$ is a singular point. Also, since statement “The functions $(x+3)P(x)$ and $(x+3)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-3.$” is false, it follows that $x=-3$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement “The point $x=-3$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation” is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The point $x=-3$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379766 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=4x-3y-1$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186379766 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=4x-3y-1$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=-1$ gives a line\n- **II.** $c=0$ gives the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$\n- **III.** $c=2$ gives a parabola" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false