nAmong the given sets, only $(-1,4)\\cup (6,7)$ is open. It consists of two parts, where the boundary points are $-1$, $4$, $6$, and $7.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not open because:\n\n- $(-2,1]\\cup [3,9)$ has four boundary points $-2$, $1$, $3$, and $9$, and the points $1$ and $3$ lie in the set.\n\n- $[4,6)\\cup (9,11)$ has four boundary points $4$, $6$, $9$, and $11$, and the point $4$ lies in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(-1,4)\\cup (6,7)$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380062 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380062 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\n- **II.** The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- **III.** The limit exists and is equal to $1$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380062 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380062 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380159 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $Q?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380159 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $Q?$\n- **I.** $¬R∧(Q∧¬R)$\n- **II.** $Q∧(¬R∨Q)$\n- **III.** $(¬R∨Q)∧Q$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380159 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nQ∧(Q∨¬R)≡Q.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $¬R∧(Q∧¬R)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $Q.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement $Q∧(¬R∨Q)$ is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q∧(¬R∨Q) & ≡Q∧(Q∨¬R) \\\\  & ≡Q.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(¬R∨Q)∧Q$ is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬R∨Q)∧Q & ≡Q∧(Q∨¬R) \\\\  & ≡Q.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Q∧(¬R∨Q)$; $(¬R∨Q)∧Q$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380159 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nQ∧(Q∨¬R)≡Q.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $Q.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q∧(¬R∨Q) & ≡Q∧(Q∨¬R) \\\\  & ≡Q.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬R∨Q)∧Q & ≡Q∧(Q∨¬R) \\\\  & ≡Q.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380166 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $P?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380166 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $P?$\n- **I.** $P∨(Q∧P)$\n- **II.** $(Q∧P)∨P$\n- **III.** $P∨(Q∨P)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380166 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nP∨(P∧Q)≡P.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $P∨(Q∧P)$ is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∧P) & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(Q∧P)∨P$ is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Q∧P)∨P & ≡P∨(Q∧P) \\\\  & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $P∨(Q∨P)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $P.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P∨(Q∧P)$; $(Q∧P)∨P$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380166 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nP∨(P∧Q)≡P.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∧P) & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Q∧P)∨P & ≡P∨(Q∧P) \\\\  & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $P.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380173 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $M?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380173 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $M?$\n- **I.** $M∨(N∨M)$\n- **II.** $(M∧N)∨M$\n- **III.** $M∨(N∧M)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380173 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nM∨(M∧N)≡M.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $M∨(N∨M)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $M.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement $(M∧N)∨M$ is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(M∧N)∨M & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $M∨(N∧M)$ is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M∨(N∧M) & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(M∧N)∨M$; $M∨(N∧M)$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380173 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nM∨(M∧N)≡M.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $M.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(M∧N)∨M & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M∨(N∧M) & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380214 1071 "$\\,$ **If** I am not sleeping, **then** I am awake.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380214 1071 "$\\,$ **If** I am not sleeping, **then** I am awake.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n- **I.** If I am not awake, then I am sleeping.\n- **II.** If I am sleeping, then I am not awake.\n- **III.** If I am awake, then I am not sleeping.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380214 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement I is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380214 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement “If I am not awake, then I am sleeping” is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “If I am sleeping, then I am not awake” is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “If I am awake, then I am not sleeping” is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If I am not awake, then I am sleeping” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380221 1071 "$\\,$ **If** we do not go to the cinema, **then** we watch a movie at home.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380221 1071 "$\\,$ **If** we do not go to the cinema, **then** we watch a movie at home.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n- **I.** If we do not watch a movie at home, then we go to the cinema.\n- **II.** If we watch a movie at home, then we do not go to the cinema.\n- **III.** If we go to the cinema, then we do not watch a movie at home.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380221 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement I is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement I is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380221 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement “If we do not watch a movie at home, then we go to the cinema” is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “If we do not watch a movie at home, then we go to the cinema” is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “If we go to the cinema, then we do not watch a movie at home” is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If we do not watch a movie at home, then we go to the cinema” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380284 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $H$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380284 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $H$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380284 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0+0=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a matrix such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0\\cdot k=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $H$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380284 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$H$ is closed under matrix addition” is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0+0=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement “$H$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. If $A$ is an element of $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a matrix such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0\\cdot k=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true, while statement “$H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. $H$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$H$ is closed under matrix addition”; “$H$ is closed under scalar multiplication”; “$H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380291 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $D={\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a<0}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380291 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $D={\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a<0}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380291 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $D,$ then  ${a}_{1}<0$ and ${a}_{2}<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n is a matrix from $D,$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}<0.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example,  if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $k=-1,$ then $A$ is an element of $D$ and $k\\in R,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* in  $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380291 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$D$ is closed under matrix addition” is true. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $D,$ then  ${a}_{1}<0$ and ${a}_{2}<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n is a matrix from $D,$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}<0.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement “$D$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. For example,  if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $k=-1,$ then $A$ is an element of $D$ and $k\\in R,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* in  $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false, while statement “$D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$D$ is closed under matrix addition”; “$D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380325 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ non-invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380325 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ non-invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n- **I.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-4\\lambda =0$\n- **II.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +4=0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380325 1074 "Since $B$ is non-invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ must be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-4\\lambda =0$ could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +4=0$ **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation $\\lambda (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0$ could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\lambda }^{3}-4\\lambda =0$; $\\lambda (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380325 1074 "Since $B$ is non-invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ must be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380338 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380338 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(B+2I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380338 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B+2I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B+2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=B+2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380338 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B+2I.$\n\n- Statement “$(B+2I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution” is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B+2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements $\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0$ are true. Since $M=B+2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$(B+2I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution”; $\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3}$; $\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0$." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380345 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380345 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(C-2I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380345 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-2I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380345 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-2I.$\n\n- Statement “$(C-2I)x=0$ has only zero solution” is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4}$ and $\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}$ are true. Since $M=C-2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4}$; $\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380352 1071 "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380352 1071 "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$\n- **II.** $B-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380352 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements “$B-2I$ is non-singular” is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement “$\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$” is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$; “$\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380352 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380393 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380393 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380393 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (1-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2-t){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-1)+(2-2)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,2-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (2-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380393 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (1-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2-t){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-1)+(2-2)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,2-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (2-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380406 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380406 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380406 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)-2)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-1)-2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =18\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380406 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\