 **III.** $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}+x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380462 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose ${2}^{1/4}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)={2}^{1/4}.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}={2}^{1/4}$ is $x=\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- II is a surjection. Given $y\\in R,$ choose $x=-\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h(-\\sqrt[3]{y})=-{(-\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3}=y.$\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ In fact ${x}^{2}+x>0$ for all $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380462 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- “$g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose ${2}^{1/4}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)={2}^{1/4}.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}={2}^{1/4}$ is $x=\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=-{x}^{3}$” is a surjection. Given $y\\in R,$ choose $x=-\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h(-\\sqrt[3]{y})=-{(-\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3}=y.$\n\n- “$f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}+x$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ In fact ${x}^{2}+x>0$ for all $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=-{x}^{3}$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380469 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186380469 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n- **I.** $f:{-1,0,1,2}\\to {-1,0,1,2,3}$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}-1$\n- **II.** $g:(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n- **III.** $h:[0,\\infty )\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)=\\sqrt{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380469 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in {-1,0,1,2,3}.$ There is no number $x\\in {-1,0,1,2}$ such that $f(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={x}^{2}-1=2$ is $x=\\sqrt{3},$ but $\\sqrt{3}\\notin {-1,0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ such that $g(x)=2.$ In fact, $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1$ for all $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Given $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={y}^{2}\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Then $h(x)=h({y}^{2})=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}}=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186380469 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, 