) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{x{sin}^{2}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{z}$ is also defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\frac{(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{(1){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only'." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381055 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{x{sin}^{2}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$” is false. If we substitute $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is defined” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{z}$ is also defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\frac{(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{(1){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is defined” only'." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381146 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381146 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381146 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is false. Since statement $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent” is true. Since statement $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$; “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381146 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement I is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381355 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-2?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381355 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-2?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The range of $f$ is $[-2,\\infty )$\n- **II.** $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- **III.** $f$ is **onto**" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381355 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8457a8caa2f9dbdb627521e23029325f9edda757336a7a5fbbffbda14b4985.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(-1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=-2$ and greater, including the number $-2,$ so the range of the function is $[-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=-3.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381355 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8457a8caa2f9dbdb627521e23029325f9edda757336a7a5fbbffbda14b4985.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The range of $f$ is $[-2,\\infty )$” is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(-1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=-2$ and greater, including the number $-2,$ so the range of the function is $[-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false. Consider $y=-3.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ is **onto**” is false. Since statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The range of $f$ is $[-2,\\infty )$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381454 1071 "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381454 1071 "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∃\\,n\\in N,\\,5\\le n.$\n- **II.** The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$\n- **III.** $∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381454 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option $∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For every natural $m$ there exists natural $n$ such that $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $∃\\,n\\in N,\\,5\\le n.$ is true since $5\\le n$ for some natural number $n$ (e.g., for $n=6$).\n\n- Statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$” is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis true for any natural $m.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=2.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=3.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m+1.$\n\n- Statement $∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$ is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ And, according to statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$”, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∃\\,n\\in N,\\,5\\le n.$; “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$”; $∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381454 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For every natural $m$ there exists natural $n$ such that $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $5\\le n$ for some natural number $n$ (e.g., for $n=6$).\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis true for any natural $m.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=2.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\