ite as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{x}-2=0$\" is the set ${4},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{2}\\in N$\" is $Z∖{0}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{4}}\\cup (Z∖{0})=(Z∖{4})\\cup (Z∖{0})=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381770 1074 "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{x}-2=0$\" is the set ${4},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{2}\\in N$\" is $Z∖{0}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{4}}\\cup (Z∖{0})=(Z∖{4})\\cup (Z∖{0})=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$” is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$” is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$ is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$”; $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186