 \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381791 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all reals $x$ and $y,$ we have $\\sqrt{xy}\\in R.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $\\sqrt{2y}\\in R$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(x)$ is false for all values $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-1.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{2y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-2.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $y=-x$ for any value of $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $R.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381805 1071 "Let $x\\in Z$ and $y\\in Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381805 1071 "Let $x\\in Z$ and $y\\in Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,x+1=0.$\n- **II.** The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)$ is $∅.$\n- **III.** $∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0.$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381805 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option $∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0.$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a integer $y$ such that, for every integer $x,$ we have $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,x+1=0.$ is false since $x+1=0$ is not true for some integer $x.$ Say, for $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)$ is $∅.$” is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(y):\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nis false for every value of $y.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(0):∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,x=0\n$$\n\nis false for any $x\\ne 0.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $x\\ne -y$ for any $y\\in Z.$ Therefore, its truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement $∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0.$ is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0}_{P(y)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(y)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)$ is $∅.$”, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)$ is $∅.$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186381805 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a integer $y$ such that, for every integer $x,$ we have $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $x+1=0$ is not true for some integer $x.$ Say, for $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(y):\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nis false for every value of $y.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(0):∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,x=0\n$$\n\nis false for any $x\\ne 0.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $x\\ne -y$ for any $y\\in Z.$ Therefore, its truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0}_{P(y)}\\,)\n