ect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- **II.** The truth set of $P(x)$ is $R.$\n- **III.** $∃x\\in R,\\,P(x).$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381860 1074 "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x<1$\" is $(-\\infty ,1),$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$|x|\\ge 1$\" is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=(-\\infty ,1)\\cap ((-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ))=(-\\infty ,-1].\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is not empty, the statement $∃x\\in R,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186381860 1074 "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x<1$\" is $(-\\infty ,1),$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$|x|\\ge 1$\" is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=(-\\infty ,1)\\cap ((-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ))=(-\\infty ,-1].\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$” is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $R.$” is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement $∃x\\in R,\\,P(x).$ is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is not empty, the statement $∃x\\in R,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∃x\\in R,\\,P(x).$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382214 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382214 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- **II.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,0)$\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(1,1)$ is defined" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382214 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1)=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,0)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(1,1)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-1}}}{2\\sqrt{1-1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II  only'." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382214 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ is defined” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1)=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,0)$” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,0)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{x}(1,1)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(1,1)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-1}}}{2\\sqrt{1-1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ is defined” and “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,0)$”  only'." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382221 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382221 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{z}(0,1,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382221 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,2)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-2}=\\sqrt{-1},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the number under the radical is negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382221 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{z}(0,1,2)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(0,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,2)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-2}=\\sqrt{-1},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the number under the radical is negative.\n\n- Statement “The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$” is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined” only'." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382303 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+1?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382303 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+1?$\n- **I.** The range of $f$ is $[1,\\infty )$\n- **II.** $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- **III.** $f$ is **onto**\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382303 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/be/bed7b280998fe987276ca5820b987f672404d52d00f532db819db1166118ed70.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $1,$ and it occurs at the point $(2,1).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=1$ and greater, including the number $1,$ so the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382303 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/be/bed7b280998fe987276ca5820b987f672404d52d00f532db819db1166118ed70.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The range of $f$ is $[1,\\infty )$” is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $1,$ and it occurs at the point $(2,1).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=1$ and greater, including the number $1,$ so the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false. Consider $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ is **onto**” is false. Since statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such tha