⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382675 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382675 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(