atorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ differentiating we have $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382675 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ differentiating we have $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382722 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=x+2?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382722 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=x+2?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(-1)\\ne f(1)$\n- **II.** $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- **III.** $f$ is an injection" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382722 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/3b/3b849edf2ae8976e0abd5e0aa43180ad61d90b01d6f229c2b7fc7898ea860400.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(-1)\\ne f(1)$ is true. We have $f(-1)=(-1)+2=1$ and $f(1)=1+2=3.$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$” is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ is an injection” is true. Indeed, since statement “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$” is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(-1)\\ne f(1)$; “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$”; “$f$ is an injection”." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382722 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/3b/3b849edf2ae8976e0abd5e0aa43180ad61d90b01d6f229c2b7fc7898ea860400.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(-1)=(-1)+2=1$ and $f(1)=1+2=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382743 1071 "![](images/8f/8f4d72bd431d4f06daa3ea2be20d0ebb21fd736b944be77331c72c2e47e3144f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[0,\\infty ]\\to (-\\infty ,5]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(0)=f(2)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [0,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382743 1071 "![](images/8f/8f4d72bd431d4f06daa3ea2be20d0ebb21fd736b944be77331c72c2e47e3144f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[0,\\infty ]\\to (-\\infty ,5]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382743 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(0)=f(2)$ is false. According to the graph, we have $f(0)=5$ and $f(2)<5.$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [0,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$” is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elem