in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is a surjection” is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is an injection” is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is a bijection” is true. Indeed, since both statements “$f$ is a surjection” and “$f$ is an injection” are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f$ is a surjection”; “$f$ is an injection”; “$f$ is a bijection”." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382778 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382778 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n- **I.** $f:{R}^{+}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- **II.** $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- **III.** $h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=|x|$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382778 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is $f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in {R}^{+}.$ So, for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ {x}^{3} & =y \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that\n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\sqrt[3]{y})={(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3}=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $h(-1)=1=h(1),$ while $-1\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382778 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- “$f:{R}^{+}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$” is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is $f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in {R}^{+}.$ So, for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- “$g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$” is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ {x}^{3} & =y \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that\n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\sqrt[3]{y})={(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3}=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- “$h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=|x|$” is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $h(-1)=1=h(1),$ while $-1\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186382785 1071 "![](images/70/707aad3ee7b52647cdc565aee76a4276aee01c8369236f24908f1c89d234e588.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R∖{-1}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=1-\\frac{1}{x+1}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186382785 1071 "![](images/70/707aad3ee7b52647cdc565aee76a4276aee01c8369236f24908