Statement $P(P({x}))={∅,{∅},{{x}},{∅,{x}}}$ is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${x}$ is ${∅,{x}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({x}))={∅,{∅},{{x}},{∅,{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement $P({{x}})={∅,{x}}$ is false. Notice that we are given the set ${{x}}.$ Then, the power set of the set ${{x}}$ is ${∅,{{x}}}.$ Therefore, $P({{x}})={∅,{{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement $P({x,∅})={∅,{x},{∅},{x,∅}}$ is true. Notice that we are given the set ${x,∅}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{x},{∅},{x,∅}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(P({x}))={∅,{∅},{{x}},{∅,{x}}}$; $P({x,∅})={∅,{x},{∅},{x,∅}}$ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186383694 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${x}$ is ${∅,{x}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({x}))={∅,{∅},{{x}},{∅,{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${{x}}.$ Then, the power set of the set ${{x}}$ is ${∅,{{x}}}.$ Therefore, $P({{x}})={∅,{{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set ${x,∅}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{x},{∅},{x,∅}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385178 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385178 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.1$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385178 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.1}| \\\\  & =0.9090…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.8}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.8}| \\\\  & =0.5555…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{6}| \\\\  & =0.1666…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385178 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.1}| \\\\  & =0.9090…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.8}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.8}| \\\\  & =0.5555…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{6}| \\\\  & =0.1666…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.1$; $\\lambda Δx=0.8$; $\\lambda Δx=1.2$; $\\lambda Δx=5$.\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385612 1071 "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385612 1071 "Which of the following functions are injections?\n- **I.** $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n- **II.** $h:[0,2\\pi ]\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- **III.** $f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385612 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(-1),$ but $1\\ne -1.$\n\n- II is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\n- III is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385612 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- “$g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$” is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(-1),$ but $1\\ne -1.$\n\n- “$h:[0,2\\pi ]\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$” is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\n- “$f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$” is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385631 1071 "For the function $f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3},$ which of the following  statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385631 1071 "For the function $f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3},$ which of the following  statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The function is an injection\n- **II.** The function is a surjection\n- **III.** The function is a bijection" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385631 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}^{3}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $f(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies ${x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385631 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is true. Indeed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}^{3}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is false. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $f(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies ${x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The function is an injection” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385638 1071 "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385638 1071 "Which of the following functions are injections?\n- **I.** $f:{1,2,3,4}\\to {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$\n- **II.** $g:{-4,-2,0,2,4,8,10}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- **III.** $h:Z\\to {-1,0,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385638 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not* an injection. For example, $g(-4)=16=g(4),$ but $-4\\ne 4.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=0=h(1),$ but $0\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385638 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- “$f:{1,2,3,4}\\to {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$” is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- “$g:{-4,-2,0,2,4,8,10}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* an injection. For example, $g(-4)=16=g(4),$ but $-4\\ne 4.$\n\n- “$h:Z\\to {-1,0,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )$” is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=0=h(1),$ but $0\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:{1,2,3,4}\\to {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385645 1071 "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385645 1071 "Which of the following functions are injections?\n- **I.** $f:{1,3,5,7,9}\\to {-4,-2,0,12}$ defined by $f(x)=(x-3)(x-7)$\n- **II.** $g:{1,2,3,4,5}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- **III.** $h:Z\\to {-1,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385645 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is *not* an injection. For example, $f(3)=0=f(7),$ but $3\\ne 7.$\n\n- II is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align