}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2),$ but $0\\ne 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385652 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- “$f:{-1,0,1,2,3}\\to Z$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$” is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- “$g:{-2,-1,0,1,2}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={2}^{x}$” is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- “$h:Z\\to {-1,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )$” is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2),$ but $0\\ne 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:{-1,0,1,2,3}\\to Z$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$”; “$g:{-2,-1,0,1,2}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={2}^{x}$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385659 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385659 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n- **I.** $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=|x|$\n- **II.** $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)=4(x-2)$\n- **III.** $h:Q\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385659 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. For example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 4(x-2) & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{4}+2\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{4}+2)=4([\\frac{y}{4}+2]-2)=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 4({x}_{1}-2) & =4({x}_{2}-2) \\\\ {x}_{1}-2 & ={x}_{2}-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385659 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- “$f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=|x|$” is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. For example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)=4(x-2)$” is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 4(x-2) & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{4}+2\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{4}+2)=4([\\frac{y}{4}+2]-2)=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 4({x}_{1}-2) & =4({x}_{2}-2) \\\\ {x}_{1}-2 & ={x}_{2}-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- “$h:Q\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$” is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)=4(x-2)$” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385685 1071 "![](images/c3/c3caf194f47cbc3fe36f969442df48e0a133ddbf77a9177e2e5e96822463c8a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(0)\\ne f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385685 1071 "![](images/c3/c3caf194f47cbc3fe36f969442df48e0a133ddbf77a9177e2e5e96822463c8a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385685 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(0)\\ne f(1)$ is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(0)=9$ (we have an arrow $0\\in X$ to $9\\in Y$) and $f(1)=0$ (we have an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $0\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$” is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ is an injection” is true. Indeed, since statement “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$” is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(0)\\ne f(1)$; “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$”; “$f$ is an injection”." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385685 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(0)=9$ (we have an arrow $0\\in X$ to $9\\in Y$) and $f(1)=0$ (we have an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $0\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385692 1071 "![](images/e1/e187742134e111e2f539fdce556c50445cc1ee8d8c6cdb93077192c14b36dfaf.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(-5)\\ne f(0)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385692 1071 "![](images/e1/e187742134e111e2f539fdce556c50445cc1ee8d8c6cdb93077192c14b36dfaf.png)\n\nWhich of the following statement