 have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is a surjection” is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is an injection” is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is a bijection” is true. Indeed, since both statements “$f$ is a surjection” and “$f$ is an injection” are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f$ is a surjection”; “$f$ is an injection”; “$f$ is a bijection”." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385782 1071 "![](images/12/126abe41ac185c082d8cc28628e2db4b2708dd84ba351718184d949ddf582d3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385782 1071 "![](images/12/126abe41ac185c082d8cc28628e2db4b2708dd84ba351718184d949ddf582d3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385782 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a biject