tion” is false (as well as statement “$f$ is an injection”), $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385789 1071 "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ which of the following  statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385789 1071 "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ which of the following  statements are true? \n- **I.** The function is an injection\n- **II.** The function is a surjection\n- **III.** The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385789 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. For instance, $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=f(2\\pi )$, but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385789 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is false. For instance, $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=f(2\\pi )$, but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385796 1071 "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}-1,$ which of the following  statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385796 1071 "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}-1,$ which of the following  statements are true? \n- **I.** The function is an injection\n- **II.** The function is a surjection\n- **III.** The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385796 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. For instance, $f(-1)=0=f(1)$, but $-1\\ne 1.$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385796 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is false. For instance, $f(-1)=0=f(1)$, but $-1\\ne 1.$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385803 1071 "For the function $f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ which of the following  statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385803 1071 "For the function $f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ which of the following  statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The function is an injection\n- **II.** The function is a surjection\n- **III.** The function is a bijection" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385803 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}})}^{3} & ={(\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}})}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, choose $\\sqrt{2}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{2}.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt{2}$ is $x=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$ Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{\\sqrt{8}}={({2}^{3/2})}^{1/3}={2}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385803 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is true. Indeed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}})}^{3} & ={(\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}})}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is false. As a counterexample, choose $\\sqrt{2}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{2}.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt{2}$ is $x=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$ Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{\\sqrt{8}}={({2}^{3/2})}^{1/3}={2}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The function is an injection” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385810 1071 "![](images/ef/ef54b0fed6fa137d1a69b35c9b0231bf21192ca062fef6246df86a32591d0c0e.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={e}^{x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385810 1071 "![](images/ef/ef54b0fed6fa137d1a69b35c9b0231bf21192ca062fef6246df86a32591d0c0e.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={e}^{x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186385810 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/bd/bda8097fd869dfc60ccdd2dd42d38072248ad91a94853474461d0454c30e8027.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$ So, there is no value $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186385810 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/bd/bda8097fd869dfc60ccdd2dd42d38072248ad91a94853474461d0454c30e8027.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is false. Indeed, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$ So, there is no value $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is “The function is an injection” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186390441 1071 "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =2\\pi $\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186390441 1071 "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =2\\pi $\n- **I.** $4\\pi $\n- **II.** $5\\pi $\n- **III.** $-2\\pi $\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186390441 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\pi =4\\pi \\,✓$\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\cdot (2\\pi )=6\\pi $\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $6\\pi .$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\pi =0$\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-2\\pi \\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ are coterminal with $2\\pi .$\n\nSo the correct answer is $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186390441 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\pi =4\\pi \\,✓$\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\cdot (2\\pi )=6\\pi $\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $6\\pi .$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\pi =0$\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-2\\pi \\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ are coterminal with $2\\pi .$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186393133 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186393133 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=1$ gives an ellipse\n- **II.** $c=2$ gives two lines\n- **III.** $c=3$ gives a hyperbola" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186393133 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-2){y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statement II is true." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186393133 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-2){y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statement “$c=2$ gives two lines” is true." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186393514 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186393514 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $t=0$\n- **II.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$\n- **III.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\n- **IV.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186394023 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186394023 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4x+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=4xy-y$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+2x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186394023 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-y)=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{y})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{