t{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  I and III are true, and II is false." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186396134 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  “Its center is $(1,-3)$” and “The length of the minor axis is $4$” are true, and “The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$” is false." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186396162 1071 "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$L=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$M=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186396162 1071 "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186396169 1071 "Which 