rom $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na={x}_{0}<{x}_{1}<{x}_{2}<…<{x}_{n}=b\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can express $P$ using set notation as\n\n$$\nP={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- Statement $-5<-4<-2<0<4<6$ is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since $-5$ does not belong to $[-4,6].$\n\n- Statement ${-3,3,5,6}$ is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the left endpoint $a=-4.$\n\n- Statement ${-4,4,5}$ is not a partition of  $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the right endpoint $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6].$\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186396627 1074 "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na={x}_{0}<{x}_{1}<{x}_{2}<…<{x}_{n}=b\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can express $P$ using set notation as\n\n$$\nP={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- Statement I is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since $-5$ does not belong to $[-4,6].$\n\n- Statement II is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the left endpoint $a=-4.$\n\n- Statement III is not a partition of  $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the right endpoint $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6].$\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186396641 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186396641 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n- **A.** $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=1}$\n- **B.** $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=1}$\n- **C.** $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186396673 1071 "![](images/e1/e111be8806fd5351b873219d9a790b71ca862a8fdef8b15df6477265cbb7ddea.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186396673 1071 "![](images/e1/e111be8806fd5351b873219d9a790b71ca862a8fdef8b15df6477265cbb7ddea.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-3$\n- **II.** $x=-2$\n- **III.** $x=0$\n- **IV.** $x=1$\n- **V.** $x=2$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186396687 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2x+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186396687 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2x+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- **II.** $x\\in (10,\\infty )$\n- **III.** $x\\in [-5,5)$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186396687 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d3/d33314230419ec762bc4ad8603b2e6c3436461cd6b4b6fe4783d12a23d01b660.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186396687 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d3/d33314230419ec762bc4ad8603b2e6c3436461cd6b4b6fe4783d12a23d01b660.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$; $x\\in (10,\\infty )$; $x\\in [-5,5)$." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399350 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z$\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399350 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399350 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z$ is divergent." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399350 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral II is divergent." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399398 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399398 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n- **II.** $y=-1$ is the maximum value of the function.\n- **III.** $x=15\\pi $ is in the domain." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399398 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ therefore the function doesn't have a maximum value. Hence, statements I and II are both false.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement III is false." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399398 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ therefore the function doesn't have a maximum value. Hence, statements “The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1].$” and “$y=-1$ is the maximum value of the function” are both false.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement “$x=15\\pi $ is in the domain” is false." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399692 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186