y." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399919 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399926 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$\nII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399926 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399954 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $-10\\,000$\nII. $\\frac{1}{16}$\nIII. $\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{6}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399954 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186399968 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186399968 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400003 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ $$\n\nIII. $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400003 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400003 1074 "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$\n\n$\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n\n$7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{7}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$ has no solutions." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400003 1074 "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{7}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400010 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $5-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nIII. $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400010 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400010 1074 "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n$5-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n\n$\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n\n$5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$ has no solutions." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400010 1074 "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III has no solutions." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400093 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400093 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400093 1074 "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $2{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence II, the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}-n)}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-n}{0-1} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence III,  the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence II diverges." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400093 1074 "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the dominant term in the denominator is $2{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}-n)}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-n}{0-1} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$” diverges." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400170 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e]?$\n\nI. $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\nII. $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\nIII. $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400170 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e]?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400170 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous at $x=0,$ which means it is not continuous on $[0,e].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e].$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400170 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous at $x=0,$ which means it is not continuous on $[0,e].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e].$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400184 1071 "Which of the following is a $2\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400184 1071 "Which of the following is a $2\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400205 1071 "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400205 1071 "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400212 1071 "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400212 1071 "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400219 1071 "Which of the following are column vectors?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400219 1071 "Which of the following are column vectors?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400324 1071 "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400324 1071 "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400331 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ . *\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\nII. $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution\nIII. $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400331 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ . *\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400331 1074 "Since $A$ is invertible, $\\operatorname{det}(A)\\ne 0.$ Also, for each vector $b,$ the equation $Ax=b$ has the unique solution $x=A^{-1}b.$ In particular, $Ax=0$ has only the zero solution. Thus statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution” are true and statement “$Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions” is false. The correct answer is $det(A)\\ne 0$; “$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution” only." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] [17592186400331 1074 "Since $A$ is invertible, $\\operatorname{det}(A)\\ne 0.$ Also, for each vector $b,$ the equation $Ax=b$ has the unique solution $x=A^{-1}b.$ In particular, $Ax=0$ has only the zero solution. Thus statements I and II are true and statement III is false. The correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537075 17592186576411 false] [17592186400338 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨{k}^{2},7,4⟩,$ $b=⟨6,7,4⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,0,2⟩$ are coplanar.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537075 17592186576411 true] 